  "slug": "aging-senescence-a873",
  "summary": "Several preprints map senescent cells in situ in human and mouse liver and human omental fat, adding p53 perturbation and causal modeling in liver.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatially resolved senescence in aging and diseased tissues, concentrated in liver. In human liver, spatial transcriptomics and scRNA-seq place senescent SenSpots at portal tracts and fibrotic septa, with phenotypes shifting during metabolic liver disease progression. A separate study applies causal discovery machine learning to Visium data to model causes and effects of senescence markers. In aged mouse liver, multi-omic mapping finds senescent cells clustered in inflammatory SenSpots with distinct epigenetic senotypes and zonation. Perturbation evidence comes from mouse liver: p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage, whereas MDM2 inhibitor-driven p53 activation is reported to reverse age-linked changes. Outside liver, human omental adipose from older adults carries more senescent cells than obesity alone explains, involving stem/progenitor and mesothelial cells. Related work links progerin to amplified inflammatory responses in iPSC-derived alveolar cells. Other items address liver injury repair and unrelated topics. All evidence is preprint-stage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
